# Copy this to vgpu.conf and edit the REQUIRED block below.
#
#     cp vgpu.conf.example vgpu.conf
#     $EDITOR vgpu.conf
#     sudo ./scripts/preflight.sh
#
# The required settings are live (not commented) because the defaults cannot be
# right for your machine - in particular there is no sensible default for the
# vGPU profile, and leaving it unset makes startguest.sh fail with
# "no vGPU profile resolved".
#
# Everything in the OPTIONAL section is commented out and already has a working
# default. Uncomment a line only when you need to change it.
#
# vgpu.conf is gitignored: it holds your PCI address and paths, so it should not
# be committed.

# ===========================================================================
# REQUIRED - edit these
# ===========================================================================

# The data filesystem holding driver trees, module sources and VM images.
# Expected layout underneath:
#   $VGPU_ROOT/driver/modules-<uname -r>/   nvidia.ko and friends, per kernel
#   $VGPU_ROOT/kmod/zfmulti/                required kprobe module
#   $VGPU_ROOT/kmod/mdguest/                required helper module
VGPU_ROOT=/srv/vgpu/VMs

# Where the guest directories live. Each guest gets its own subdirectory
# holding its qcow2 and its own OVMF_VARS.fd.
VM_BASE=/var/lib/vgpu-vm

# The vGPU profile to give the guest. THIS HAS NO DEFAULT.
#
# Use the NAME, not the nvidia-NNN type id: the numeric id is assigned per
# driver version and per board and will not match yours. preflight.sh lists
# every profile your card offers, with framebuffer sizes and availability.
#
# RTXA5000-8Q is what a GeForce RTX 3090 presents when spoofed to an A5000.
VGPU_PROFILE_NAME=RTXA5000-8Q

# The guest. VM_DISK is relative to $VM_BASE/$VM_NAME/, so this expects
#   /var/lib/vgpu-vm/winguest/win.qcow2
#   /var/lib/vgpu-vm/winguest/OVMF_VARS.fd
VM_NAME=winguest
VM_DISK=win.qcow2

# ===========================================================================
# OPTIONAL - every line below is already the working default
# ===========================================================================

# --- paths -----------------------------------------------------------------

# Scripts append here rather than truncating.
#LOG_DIR=/var/log/vgpu

# --- GPU -------------------------------------------------------------------

# PCI address of the GPU, domain included. Auto-detected from sysfs when
# unset. Set it explicitly only if the machine has more than one NVIDIA card -
# detection deliberately refuses to guess between them.
#   lspci -D -d 10de::0300
#GPU_BDF=0000:0a:00.0

# Only consulted if VGPU_PROFILE_NAME above matches nothing. Hard-codes the
# type directory instead of resolving by name.
#VGPU_TYPE=nvidia-664

# Written to the mdev's nvidia/vgpu_params before the guest starts.
#VGPU_PARAMS=loglevel=5,disable_vnc=1

# Module parameters for nvidia.ko. RMSetSriovMode=0 selects the legacy mdev
# path, which is the only one consumer Ampere can use. GSP cannot boot on this
# card, so the firmware stays off.
#NVIDIA_REGISTRY_DWORDS=RMSetSriovMode=0
#NVIDIA_ENABLE_GPU_FIRMWARE=0

# --- the one required intervention -----------------------------------------

# zfmulti forces a zero flag at one branch in the RM interrupt handler. That
# branch otherwise parks the graphics runlist with nothing re-enabling it, and
# the guest's graphics channel is scheduled once and then never again.
#
# This spec is tied to the exact driver BUILD. The symbol name is anonymised by
# NVIDIA and both the name and the offset move between driver versions. On
# 535.309.01 it is _nv042311rm+0x28. On any other build you must locate it
# yourself. docs/FINDING-THE-ISR-OFFSET.md walks it through, and kmod/isrfind
# automates the call-site attribution that used to be done by hand.
#ZFMULTI_SPEC=_nv042311rm+0x28:1

# --- guest sizing ----------------------------------------------------------

#VM_MEM_MB=12288
#VM_CORES=4
#VM_THREADS=2

# OVMF firmware. Searched across the common distribution paths when unset.
# Must pair with your 4MB OVMF_VARS.fd - a 2MB code image will not boot
# against it. Note "4m" names the CODE+VARS layout, not the file size.
#OVMF_CODE=/usr/share/edk2/x64/OVMF_CODE.4m.fd

# --- network exposure ------------------------------------------------------

# Host port forwarded to the guest's RDP port 3389. 0 disables the forward.
#RDP_HOST_PORT=3390

# QEMU's own VNC display number, so you can see the guest before RDP is up.
# Display N listens on port 5900+N. Set to -1 to disable.
#QEMU_VNC_DISPLAY=2

# Address the QEMU VNC console binds to. Loopback by default: that console is
# an unauthenticated keyboard, mouse and screen for the guest, so putting it on
# 0.0.0.0 hands guest control to anyone who can reach the host. Tunnel instead:
#   ssh -L 5902:127.0.0.1:5902 user@host
#QEMU_VNC_BIND=127.0.0.1

# Address the forwarded RDP port binds to. Loopback for the same reason, even
# though RDP itself is authenticated.
#RDP_BIND=127.0.0.1

# --- systemd unit names ----------------------------------------------------

# Override if your driver packaging names them differently, for example a
# versioned pair installed alongside a distribution driver.
#VGPUD_UNIT=nvidia-vgpud
#VGPU_MGR_UNIT=nvidia-vgpu-mgr

# --- kernel allow-list -----------------------------------------------------

# Kernels this has actually been made to work on. preflight.sh warns loudly
# when the running kernel is not in this list, because the failure mode on an
# untested kernel is a slow one: the stack loads, the guest boots, and the
# framebuffer blit times out thousands of times.
#KERNEL_KNOWN_GOOD="5.15.95 6.1.71"
